WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.970
So who's my guest this week?

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:04.570
The British lawmaker and activist,

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:07.000
Baroness Jane Campbell.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.520
When you've originally been
given a life expectancy

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:13.400
of two years, you know how to
make the most of your time.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.010
Born with spinal muscular
atrophy in an era

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:18.320
when the world was an
even harsher environment

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.040
for those with disabilities,

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.380
she's fought politicians around the globe,

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.410
challenging segregated schools,

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:27.610
battling for access to
transport, independent living,

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:30.030
and then became a politician herself.

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:32.930
She was made a Baroness
in the UK Parliament,

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:35.440
blocking laws for euthanasia,

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.000
even in the face of some
passionate advocates

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.950
who are pleading to die.

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:42.180
If I should ever seek death,

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:47.150
the last thing I want is
for you to give up on me.

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:50.940
She says disability isn't
her problem, it's society's,

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:53.840
and she will change society.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:55.280
Here's a taste.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:57.930
What is top of your list to achieve

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:00.270
for those living with
disabilities around the world?

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:02.684
I just want them to feel alive

00:01:02.684 --> 00:01:07.290
and I want to enable them, in some way,

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:11.271
to feel empowered, because
once you feel empowered

00:01:11.271 --> 00:01:14.786
and you have a bit of
confidence, you can do anything.

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:29.000
Hello, thank you so much for talking to me

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:30.510
and to all of us today.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:32.950
Hello Emma. I'm very pleased to be here.

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:37.030
I wanted to start by asking
about spinal muscular atrophy

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:40.720
and what it means to you
as someone living with it?

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:44.870
To be absolutely honest, I
don't really think about it.

00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:47.935
I don't wake up in the morning and think,

00:01:47.935 --> 00:01:49.107
"Oh, I've got spinal muscular atrophy.

00:01:49.107 --> 00:01:52.710
What's gonna happen with my body today?"

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:56.530
I just tend to get on with life.

00:01:56.530 --> 00:01:59.570
I've lived with it since I was born,

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:01.640
it's part of who I am.

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:06.170
My impairment doesn't define me,

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:09.010
but it certainly is a reason

00:02:09.010 --> 00:02:13.960
for why I am sitting with you here today,

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:16.590
talking about some of the things I do.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:21.590
Because my impairment, my condition,

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:23.560
whatever you want to call it,

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:26.450
is something that I'm just used to.

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:29.460
It's my friend, it can be my enemy,

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:32.443
it gives me a hard time sometimes,

00:02:32.443 --> 00:02:35.040
and it is progressive.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.170
So I have to keep up with it

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:41.400
and keep inventing new ways to do things.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:45.720
And when people, if they
say to you, "What is wrong?"

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:48.280
If they even put it like that
to you, do they say that?

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:49.113
All the time.

00:02:49.113 --> 00:02:51.860
Right, so what do you say?

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:54.040
I recognize that your
relationship with it's different

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.260
and you've only known this.
Absolutely.

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:59.940
Well, it depends on what kind
of mood I'm in, to be honest.

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:01.873
If somebody comes up and they're young

00:03:01.873 --> 00:03:05.860
and they're, you know, knowledgeable

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:07.900
and I think they should know better

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:10.787
and they say, "So what's
wrong with you, then?"

00:03:10.787 --> 00:03:14.060
I normally go, "Well, I'm
a bit broke at the minute,

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:16.910
but apart from that, I'm fine.

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:18.460
What's wrong with you?"

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:22.010
And then they'll go, "Well,
no, no, no. I don't mean that."

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:24.950
And I go, "Oh, what do you mean?"

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:27.610
And if they want to pursue it,

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:30.620
but by this time I hope
they get the message,

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:34.430
they will ask me, "No,
what's your condition?"

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:36.432
You know, "Why are you in a wheelchair?"

00:03:36.432 --> 00:03:41.432
And I go, "Um, well, that
depends. Why do you want to know?

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:44.120
What would you like me to tell you?"

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:49.120
Because I want people to think
what is behind that question.

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:51.660
Why do they want to know?

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:54.020
I was going to ask about
assumptions people make,

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:56.780
and perhaps some of the
more frustrating ones

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:01.780
that you just wish weren't
part of how you were perceived.

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:04.910
I think they assume that I must be in pain

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:09.620
or that I must be upset
that I'm in a wheelchair

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:13.933
or that my life doesn't
have the same things in it

00:04:13.933 --> 00:04:16.183
than theirs does.

00:04:16.183 --> 00:04:19.700
They think that, for some
reason, I don't go to the pub

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:24.700
or I don't go to see a
performance of "Swan Lake"

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:26.980
at the Royal Opera House.

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:29.163
Because how would I? I'm in a wheelchair.

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:31.650
That still happens.

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:35.760
It's a lot, lot better, but, you know,

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:39.550
in my youth, I couldn't go into a cinema

00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:42.300
because people thought I was a fire risk.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:45.390
You know, somehow I wouldn't get out

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:49.640
and I could get out the cinema
probably quicker than you.

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:52.460
It was your dad, I
understand, who never took no

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:53.320
for an answer for you.

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:57.733
No, my dad was more
bloody minded than I am,

00:04:57.733 --> 00:05:01.845
and he was always determined

00:05:01.845 --> 00:05:06.350
that I would be treated
exactly the same as my sister,

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:10.840
which was tough on me at times.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:14.280
You know, I had to do all
the same things as she did,

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:16.880
you know, chores around the house.

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:19.940
Okay, I couldn't take them tea in bed,

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:21.840
but I could do the wiping up.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:25.520
I was treated equally at home.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:29.390
I wasn't treated equally
anywhere else, but I was at home.

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:34.107
So he would drag me and
my sister to museums

00:05:34.107 --> 00:05:38.280
and parks and the circus

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:41.900
and he'd even hoick me
up on the merry-go-rounds

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:45.150
and in the bumper cars at the fun fair,

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:48.058
because he wanted me to experience

00:05:48.058 --> 00:05:52.225
what it was like to be just ordinary.

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:56.310
And he pushed me hard.

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:58.980
Well, we are hearing some
of the fruits of that

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:01.200
in terms of where you've got
to and what you've been able

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:03.620
to do, which we'll also
come into more detail.

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:07.250
But just something you said
about getting to people's homes

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:08.700
and if they asked you what they could do,

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:10.910
it made me think of I read a recent report

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:13.970
of someone preparing to
take a refugee from Ukraine

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:17.150
and they are repurposing
their home for a family

00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:20.580
who have a disabled son,
he's in a wheelchair,

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:22.000
living with disabilities.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.590
And I wonder what your reflections
are thinking about that,

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:28.210
because when we think about
war, we don't necessarily think

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:30.170
about those who are
living with disabilities.

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:34.917
Yeah, I think the first
moments that we were hearing

00:06:34.917 --> 00:06:38.170
about what was happening in Ukraine,

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:43.140
I immediately thought about
the 2.7 million disabled people

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:45.200
that were living in Ukraine.

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:47.230
I was thinking, "Well, what would I do

00:06:47.230 --> 00:06:49.770
if suddenly I couldn't be in my home,

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:52.033
and I was down in a tube station

00:06:52.033 --> 00:06:55.097
and there was nowhere to
plug my ventilator into?

00:06:55.097 --> 00:06:56.380
I would be dead."

00:06:56.380 --> 00:07:00.150
So this war is going

00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:05.030
to profoundly affect the
lives of disabled people

00:07:05.030 --> 00:07:10.030
and those who are at the most
vulnerable edge of society.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:15.060
And we already know that
there's a lot of children

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:17.773
living in some of their residential homes,

00:07:17.773 --> 00:07:21.110
because they do still have
a lot of residential homes

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:24.070
where kids are shut away.

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:26.767
Some of them, I have heard,

00:07:26.767 --> 00:07:28.697
have been just left there to die.

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:32.940
Because, you know, in many ways

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:35.597
it's the survival of the fittest

00:07:35.597 --> 00:07:39.203
and I'm afraid that is true.

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:43.520
We don't like to think about it.

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:46.070
We all think about compassion

00:07:46.070 --> 00:07:50.970
when it comes to, to disabled
people, to older people,

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:53.860
but there is a dark side of all that

00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:56.966
to where there isn't the compassion,

00:07:56.966 --> 00:08:01.966
to where actually vulnerable
people of any sort

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:07.130
are subject to bullying and left

00:08:07.130 --> 00:08:08.910
to fend for themselves.

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:11.750
Yeah, I haven't heard
anybody talk about that.

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:12.590
No, you won't.

00:08:12.590 --> 00:08:14.930
We don't even talk about
it in our own country.

00:08:14.930 --> 00:08:17.935
No, but with regards to
war, in this most recent

00:08:17.935 --> 00:08:18.987
situation.
Yeah, well that's what

00:08:18.987 --> 00:08:23.372
I'm trying to find out at
the moment through the UN.

00:08:23.372 --> 00:08:27.380
You have the UN Committee on
the Rights of Disabled Persons,

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:29.840
and they have set up some charities

00:08:30.910 --> 00:08:33.600
directly for this purpose of finding out

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:37.538
where are they, are they still
alive, and how can we help?

00:08:37.538 --> 00:08:42.538
It's a very important area
of work to keep going with,

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:44.235
even in war.
Absolutely.

00:08:44.235 --> 00:08:45.899
But I think absolutely the
idea of survival of the fittest

00:08:45.899 --> 00:08:47.850
is something I'll definitely remember

00:08:47.850 --> 00:08:49.162
when we're thinking about this,

00:08:49.162 --> 00:08:51.100
'cause I hadn't thought
about it in those terms,

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:53.460
even though I was thinking
about just this family,

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:55.173
trying to prepare their home

00:08:55.173 --> 00:08:57.835
for a wheelchair.
Good on that family.

00:08:57.835 --> 00:08:59.980
I would want to go and give them a hug,

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:03.050
because not many people
would think like that.

00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:05.360
And the survival element has been key,

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:06.520
especially when, as you say,

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:10.700
you are up against a
deteriorating situation

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:13.047
in your own body that you're
trying to keep ahead of

00:09:13.047 --> 00:09:15.850
and you made the decision
to be fed by a tube.

00:09:15.850 --> 00:09:19.030
It's a big change in your life.

00:09:19.030 --> 00:09:20.710
It was, yes.
How has that been?

00:09:20.710 --> 00:09:23.330
And I suppose there must be things,

00:09:23.330 --> 00:09:25.930
like you miss out on the
social side, in some ways,

00:09:25.930 --> 00:09:28.350
of dinner at first, or you
may be concerned about that.

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:29.183
Tell me about that.

00:09:29.183 --> 00:09:33.637
I mean, there's a lot of cultural norms

00:09:33.637 --> 00:09:35.720
around sharing food.

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:37.327
You get invited to dinner,

00:09:37.327 --> 00:09:41.140
you have work dinners,
you have social dinners,

00:09:41.140 --> 00:09:45.312
food, food, food, and suddenly,

00:09:45.312 --> 00:09:47.940
food wasn't gonna become an option for me.

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:50.320
And I liked food and I
loved going out to dinner

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:53.360
and I loved socializing.

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:56.520
You probably found out I'm
a bit of a chatter box,

00:09:56.520 --> 00:09:57.990
so I enjoy all that.

00:09:57.990 --> 00:09:59.330
But it wasn't going to do me any good.

00:09:59.330 --> 00:10:02.150
In fact, it was gonna kill me in the end.

00:10:02.150 --> 00:10:05.309
So it was something I had to get used to.

00:10:05.309 --> 00:10:08.212
I went through a period
of loss, of course I did.

00:10:08.212 --> 00:10:13.212
But I can tell you, what, nine years on,

00:10:13.940 --> 00:10:16.300
I don't even think about eating food.

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:20.850
Now, strangely enough, I
still like cooking food.

00:10:20.850 --> 00:10:23.090
So I like cooking for my friends,

00:10:23.090 --> 00:10:26.670
I like going to dinner, to where food is,

00:10:26.670 --> 00:10:29.410
but what I did have to do,

00:10:29.410 --> 00:10:33.750
because all the invitations
immediately dropped off,

00:10:33.750 --> 00:10:37.235
so suddenly I thought, "Well
this is half my social life

00:10:37.235 --> 00:10:38.917
has suddenly come to an end."

00:10:38.917 --> 00:10:40.780
And I wasn't gonna have that,

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:44.422
so some of my friends, I said,

00:10:44.422 --> 00:10:48.067
"Oh, I didn't get invited to your dinner."

00:10:48.067 --> 00:10:51.872
And they said, "Well, you know,
we can't bear to sit there

00:10:51.872 --> 00:10:54.900
and eat in front of you,
'cause we feel for you."

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:59.780
And I go, "Well, actually I
feel worse when you exclude me.

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:03.640
So I'll come to dinner,
and put a nice glass

00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:06.797
of Chardonnay on the
table, and I'll be fine.

00:11:06.797 --> 00:11:09.922
In fact, make it Champagne,
I'll be even better."

00:11:09.922 --> 00:11:12.399
So I'll sit,

00:11:12.399 --> 00:11:16.097
and I'll drink, 'cause I can easily drink.

00:11:16.097 --> 00:11:18.910
Coffee, I've bought myself
a good coffee machine

00:11:18.910 --> 00:11:23.280
and I'm love with coffee
and all kinds of drinks.

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:25.880
And ice cream, I can still eat ice cream.

00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:26.840
And I'll be fine.

00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:30.900
And again, it was, I had to reassure them.

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:33.530
You said about, if somebody asked you,

00:11:33.530 --> 00:11:35.310
if I asked you about your condition,

00:11:35.310 --> 00:11:38.390
what would it actually tell you about you.

00:11:38.390 --> 00:11:42.400
So much of your life, you don't
wish to be defined by this.

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:46.670
And yet your work has made
you extremely well known

00:11:46.670 --> 00:11:50.430
in this area about rights
for others, equality.

00:11:50.430 --> 00:11:53.640
How do you marry that up,
not wanting to be defined,

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:56.940
but so much of your work is around access

00:11:56.940 --> 00:11:58.920
for those living with disabilities?

00:11:58.920 --> 00:12:03.190
Well, I suppose because I am not defined

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:07.202
to being just a disabled person,

00:12:07.202 --> 00:12:10.340
talking about my own experience.

00:12:10.340 --> 00:12:14.423
I'm interested in equality for all.

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:19.170
Typically, I don't just work on issues

00:12:19.170 --> 00:12:23.453
to do with disability,
I support my colleagues

00:12:23.453 --> 00:12:27.289
who are doing work around race equality,

00:12:27.289 --> 00:12:31.579
and I do like working with
a lot of women's groups,

00:12:31.579 --> 00:12:35.602
because I actually think
that intersectionality

00:12:35.602 --> 00:12:40.579
is incredibly important,
because I could be a Black woman

00:12:40.579 --> 00:12:42.580
or I could be a Black disabled woman.

00:12:42.580 --> 00:12:47.580
So, there are different
parts of you that, basically,

00:12:50.450 --> 00:12:53.060
they speak to different characteristics.

00:12:53.060 --> 00:12:56.610
So one day I can be sitting
around the board table

00:12:56.610 --> 00:13:00.563
with some of my colleagues
and they can ignore me,

00:13:00.563 --> 00:13:03.960
not because I'm a disabled
person, but because I'm a woman

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:07.820
and I'm sitting with some very, very male,

00:13:07.820 --> 00:13:12.820
stereotypically male,
characters, and I have to really

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:16.423
be forceful in order
to get my point across.

00:13:16.423 --> 00:13:18.800
I think I prefer to say that I work

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:20.593
in the field of human rights.

00:13:21.510 --> 00:13:23.183
And I just want human rights-

00:13:23.183 --> 00:13:25.160
Well I think that speaks-
For everybody.

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:27.070
I was gonna say, that speaks very closely

00:13:27.070 --> 00:13:30.070
to one of the campaigns that
you are very well known for

00:13:30.070 --> 00:13:31.310
around the world.

00:13:31.310 --> 00:13:33.780
But if you look at the UK,
which is where you have a role

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:36.730
in in the legislation, for
blocking assisted dying

00:13:36.730 --> 00:13:39.630
becoming law in the UK.
Yeah, it's a tough one.

00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:40.740
Others have worked with you, of course,

00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:43.650
but most recently you did this last year

00:13:43.650 --> 00:13:46.190
and it does keep coming back.

00:13:46.190 --> 00:13:48.760
It's not something that
goes away for a while

00:13:48.760 --> 00:13:52.610
and countries around the world
have moved to legalize it.

00:13:52.610 --> 00:13:54.650
Why are you against it?

00:13:54.650 --> 00:13:58.970
I think if we lived in a
world that treated people,

00:13:58.970 --> 00:14:02.910
sick people and disabled people like me,

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:06.680
equally with respect and not with fear,

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:09.422
then I would feel quite comfortable

00:14:09.422 --> 00:14:14.049
about having assisted suicide legislation.

00:14:14.049 --> 00:14:19.020
But the truth is that, and
we just know this by looking

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:21.250
at our recent pandemic.

00:14:21.250 --> 00:14:24.480
Within the pandemic, disabled people

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:28.930
disproportionately suffered
as a result of the pandemic.

00:14:28.930 --> 00:14:31.730
It was extraordinary, nearly
6 out of every 10 people

00:14:31.730 --> 00:14:34.330
who died with COVID in England

00:14:34.330 --> 00:14:37.780
were disabled or underlying
health conditions were given

00:14:37.780 --> 00:14:40.390
as the pretext for that.

00:14:40.390 --> 00:14:42.470
But, I mean, it was extraordinary.

00:14:42.470 --> 00:14:45.985
It wasn't because we were
more subject to getting COVID.

00:14:45.985 --> 00:14:47.949
In fact, when colds are going around,

00:14:47.949 --> 00:14:49.873
I probably am the last one to get one,

00:14:49.873 --> 00:14:52.420
'cause I'm very robust.

00:14:52.420 --> 00:14:56.670
It's because we didn't
have the same access

00:14:56.670 --> 00:14:59.643
to intensive care or ventilators.

00:15:01.199 --> 00:15:05.240
Even one of my friends, who
is also a ventilator user,

00:15:06.570 --> 00:15:10.022
was told she couldn't have
filters for her ventilator

00:15:10.022 --> 00:15:12.599
because they had to be kept for people

00:15:12.599 --> 00:15:17.599
who were fit and well and getting COVID.

00:15:17.710 --> 00:15:22.090
And it's true that, when I went,

00:15:22.090 --> 00:15:25.910
I found I had bowel
cancer three years ago,

00:15:25.910 --> 00:15:28.830
and it was quite serious
and I needed an operation

00:15:28.830 --> 00:15:33.790
there and then, my
consultant had to basically

00:15:33.790 --> 00:15:36.900
advocate for me to have that operation,

00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:41.900
because the view is that
actually I'm at end of life,

00:15:42.653 --> 00:15:45.700
that this is what I'm defined as,

00:15:45.700 --> 00:15:48.225
because I have a progressive impairment

00:15:48.225 --> 00:15:49.930
and that I was less likely-
So what, perhaps,

00:15:49.930 --> 00:15:52.175
you wouldn't want to be
operated on for bowel cancer?

00:15:52.175 --> 00:15:53.211
"Well, why would we operate

00:15:53.211 --> 00:15:56.277
on her, because she's
likely to die on the table."

00:15:57.390 --> 00:15:59.747
Why? I don't know why.

00:15:59.747 --> 00:16:02.680
"And also her recovery, you know,

00:16:02.680 --> 00:16:06.430
what would her life be
back like after recovery?"

00:16:06.430 --> 00:16:09.580
And it's a lot of clinical assumptions.

00:16:09.580 --> 00:16:12.847
Again, my mother was told, you know,

00:16:12.847 --> 00:16:15.360
"Your child won't live till she's two.

00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:16.960
Go home and enjoy her."

00:16:16.960 --> 00:16:21.290
Well, this month I
celebrate my 63rd birthday.

00:16:21.290 --> 00:16:26.110
So I know that there are
so many assumptions made.

00:16:26.110 --> 00:16:28.910
So is that your fear that, you know,

00:16:28.910 --> 00:16:32.559
there's a kind of euthanasia
policy there that isn't being

00:16:32.559 --> 00:16:34.534
advertised?
Oh, I know there is.

00:16:34.534 --> 00:16:36.593
There's plenty of examples.

00:16:36.593 --> 00:16:40.270
I could tell you of a time, 15 years ago,

00:16:40.270 --> 00:16:44.200
when I had pneumonia and
I had to go to a hospital

00:16:44.200 --> 00:16:46.497
that doesn't know me, and they said,

00:16:46.497 --> 00:16:48.850
"Well, we're not going to resuscitate her,

00:16:48.850 --> 00:16:51.960
because in her condition,
it wouldn't be fair."

00:16:51.960 --> 00:16:53.460
Was this said to a family member?

00:16:53.460 --> 00:16:57.423
It was said to my husband,
and my husband had to go home,

00:16:57.423 --> 00:17:01.172
and I'd just received a
doctorate, an honorary doctorate

00:17:01.172 --> 00:17:02.670
for some work that I had done,

00:17:02.670 --> 00:17:06.500
and he brought the picture back
of me in my graduation gown.

00:17:06.500 --> 00:17:10.640
He said, "This is my
wife. She works full time.

00:17:10.640 --> 00:17:14.040
You do for her everything
else that you would do

00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:18.150
for anyone else who
has that kind of life."

00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:20.480
You know, "She's in her 50s.

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:23.146
Why aren't you giving her antibiotics?

00:17:23.146 --> 00:17:24.846
Why wouldn't you resuscitate her?"

00:17:25.790 --> 00:17:28.880
And it was only at that
point they began to see me

00:17:28.880 --> 00:17:32.057
in a different light, and
it was all that load of,

00:17:32.057 --> 00:17:36.710
"Oh, she's got SMA, she's on a ventilator,

00:17:36.710 --> 00:17:38.820
she must be end of life."

00:17:38.820 --> 00:17:40.763
So to be clear, though,

00:17:41.690 --> 00:17:43.890
those are incredibly worrying examples,

00:17:43.890 --> 00:17:45.200
and those are only from your life.

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:47.940
You will have heard many, many others.

00:17:47.940 --> 00:17:50.630
But for those who, and you
will have spoken to them,

00:17:50.630 --> 00:17:54.310
who are in a situation where
they really do want to die,

00:17:54.310 --> 00:17:57.120
they're in an enormous amount of pain,

00:17:57.120 --> 00:18:01.630
it isn't a life for them
anymore, as far as they see it-

00:18:01.630 --> 00:18:04.550
Absolutely.
and they need assistance.

00:18:04.550 --> 00:18:07.560
How do you speak to them about your view

00:18:07.560 --> 00:18:09.500
and the role you've played

00:18:09.500 --> 00:18:11.650
blocking what they want to become law?

00:18:11.650 --> 00:18:14.390
What I say is, at the moment,

00:18:14.390 --> 00:18:17.580
there are not the safeguards.

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:20.863
There is not the kinds
of attitudes that we know

00:18:20.863 --> 00:18:24.770
that would safeguard
thousands upon thousands

00:18:24.770 --> 00:18:27.959
of disabled and older
people in this country

00:18:27.959 --> 00:18:31.360
who need palliative care
and who need the support

00:18:31.360 --> 00:18:35.110
to live with dignity and not in pain.

00:18:35.110 --> 00:18:40.110
And until that's there,
if we brought in this bill

00:18:40.180 --> 00:18:43.303
for these one or two exceptional cases,

00:18:44.620 --> 00:18:49.446
we will be putting hundreds
of people in dire risk.

00:18:49.446 --> 00:18:50.580
I mean, this-
We should say

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:53.522
you were a protestor, you
know, long before you were in-

00:18:53.522 --> 00:18:55.810
I was.
And you may still feel

00:18:55.810 --> 00:18:58.580
you are, but I meant that was
very much what you were doing

00:18:58.580 --> 00:19:00.680
for a long time.
Yeah, but I was doing

00:19:00.680 --> 00:19:03.820
women's rights before I was
doing disability rights.

00:19:03.820 --> 00:19:06.450
Well, I didn't just say
in this area, though.

00:19:06.450 --> 00:19:08.680
You were a protester. That was
a lot of what you were doing.

00:19:08.680 --> 00:19:10.430
What was the naughtiest
thing you got up to

00:19:10.430 --> 00:19:12.570
as a protestor that you
wouldn't be able to do now

00:19:12.570 --> 00:19:15.350
as a member of the House of Lords?

00:19:15.350 --> 00:19:19.290
I took 50 wheelchair users.

00:19:19.290 --> 00:19:23.740
We made sure that most of us
were in electric wheelchairs,

00:19:23.740 --> 00:19:25.882
'cause you cannot lift them,

00:19:25.882 --> 00:19:30.882
and we just crossed Westminster
Bridge and we stopped.

00:19:31.247 --> 00:19:33.590
And we stayed there
for an hour and a half,

00:19:33.590 --> 00:19:38.410
and we got little placards out
saying, "Rights Not Charity,"

00:19:38.410 --> 00:19:40.990
because we wanted civil rights.

00:19:40.990 --> 00:19:44.180
We didn't want to be
seen as charity cases,

00:19:44.180 --> 00:19:49.180
that we had no civil rights,
no right to go in any building,

00:19:49.535 --> 00:19:53.033
no right to stay in your own home,

00:19:53.033 --> 00:19:57.730
they could put you in a
residential home at any point.

00:19:57.730 --> 00:20:00.485
No rights, absolutely none.

00:20:00.485 --> 00:20:03.320
Not to go on a bus, not to do anything.

00:20:03.320 --> 00:20:05.800
And so that's when it all began.

00:20:05.800 --> 00:20:10.348
And I just thought it was
the best day of my life.

00:20:10.348 --> 00:20:12.877
And what did the police do?

00:20:12.877 --> 00:20:16.130
They didn't know whether to arrest us

00:20:16.130 --> 00:20:17.950
or give us an ice cream

00:20:17.950 --> 00:20:19.900
and pat us on the head.

00:20:19.900 --> 00:20:23.598
To be absolutely honest,
they were bewildered,

00:20:23.598 --> 00:20:26.168
because here are all
these, what they thought

00:20:26.168 --> 00:20:29.111
were poor pathetic creatures,

00:20:29.111 --> 00:20:30.910
sitting in the middle of the road,

00:20:30.910 --> 00:20:33.410
going, "Wotcha Officer?"

00:20:33.410 --> 00:20:35.998
And they would say,
"Would you please move?"

00:20:35.998 --> 00:20:39.610
And we went, "Sorry, battery's dead."

00:20:39.610 --> 00:20:42.709
And we were just winding them up,

00:20:42.709 --> 00:20:46.510
and they kind of quite liked it.

00:20:46.510 --> 00:20:50.950
The second time we did it,
they were a bit more prepared.

00:20:50.950 --> 00:20:54.340
So they walked us down
to the police station,

00:20:54.340 --> 00:20:57.825
but of course it was
two steps up to the door

00:20:57.825 --> 00:20:59.761
so we couldn't get in.

00:20:59.761 --> 00:21:02.927
And so they tried to get us in.

00:21:02.927 --> 00:21:05.520
You know, it was difficult.

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:07.580
There were some deaf people with us,

00:21:07.580 --> 00:21:12.135
they demanded a sign language interpreter,

00:21:12.135 --> 00:21:14.646
someone else wanted something in braille,

00:21:14.646 --> 00:21:17.440
and in the end, they just said,
"For God's sake, just go."

00:21:17.440 --> 00:21:22.440
And this was our time, and it
was a very liberating moment.

00:21:24.330 --> 00:21:29.330
And that's why we often talk
about the disability movement

00:21:29.360 --> 00:21:31.987
and they say, "Well, what was it?"

00:21:31.987 --> 00:21:35.810
And I said it was our liberation struggle,

00:21:35.810 --> 00:21:40.810
just like women had many years ago,

00:21:40.870 --> 00:21:43.630
and we had to go through that period

00:21:43.630 --> 00:21:46.835
of being suffragettes and throwing bricks

00:21:46.835 --> 00:21:49.270
or sitting in the road.

00:21:49.270 --> 00:21:52.398
But then it was time to negotiate,

00:21:52.398 --> 00:21:55.520
and I think that's when
I became very interested

00:21:55.520 --> 00:21:59.111
in politics and the political machine.

00:21:59.111 --> 00:22:02.970
'Cause I knew, at the end of
the day, we needed legislation.

00:22:02.970 --> 00:22:07.110
I wanted to ask, what is
top of your list to achieve

00:22:07.110 --> 00:22:09.420
for of those living with
disabilities around the world,

00:22:09.420 --> 00:22:13.853
if you could get one thing
really in a better place?

00:22:14.890 --> 00:22:19.890
I want them to feel personally
in control of their lives.

00:22:20.170 --> 00:22:25.170
I want them to believe that
they have life chances,

00:22:26.440 --> 00:22:30.500
that they can get a job, or
if they feel they can't work,

00:22:30.500 --> 00:22:34.600
that they can contribute in some way,

00:22:34.600 --> 00:22:38.673
whether that's artistically,
whether that's socially.

00:22:38.673 --> 00:22:43.110
I just want disabled
people not to sit at home,

00:22:43.110 --> 00:22:48.024
watching daytime TV, and
feeling sorry for themselves,

00:22:48.024 --> 00:22:52.053
because that makes them socially dead.

00:22:52.053 --> 00:22:54.448
I just want them to feel alive.

00:22:54.448 --> 00:22:59.080
And I want to enable them, in some way,

00:22:59.080 --> 00:23:03.090
to feel empowered, because
once you feel empowered

00:23:03.090 --> 00:23:06.990
and you have a bit of
confidence, you can do anything.

00:23:06.990 --> 00:23:08.280
Honestly, you can.
Well, yeah,

00:23:08.280 --> 00:23:09.990
Even block the road

00:23:09.990 --> 00:23:12.199
with a group of your friends.
Yeah, stop the traffic.

00:23:12.199 --> 00:23:14.404
I did promise that I
wouldn't do it anymore

00:23:14.404 --> 00:23:17.770
when I went into parliament.

00:23:17.770 --> 00:23:18.660
Baroness Jane Campbell,

00:23:18.660 --> 00:23:21.330
thank you so much for talking
to me and to all of us.

00:23:21.330 --> 00:23:22.387
Well thank you, Emma.

00:23:22.387 --> 00:23:25.300
It's been a real pleasure. Thank you.

00:23:25.300 --> 00:23:27.850
And thank you so much for being with us.

00:23:27.850 --> 00:23:30.703
Until we meet again,
take care and goodbye.

